  <section class="overture paper" id="before">
    {% renderTemplate "webc" %}
      <chapter-marker number="00" label="Before the number"></chapter-marker>
    {% endrenderTemplate %}
    <div class="overture__grid">
      <p class="overture__lede reveal">Before a day was a square on a screen, it was a change in the light.</p>
      <div class="prose reveal">
        <p>Morning entered the room. A shadow crossed a stone. A flower opened, a bird returned, a river rose and then receded. Time did not announce itself as a number. It arrived as alteration, as the world becoming slightly different from what it had been.</p>
        <p>To make a calendar is to gather these changes and give them edges. It is to say: this return belongs with that return; this moon begins something; this harvest closes it. A calendar looks like arithmetic, but underneath the numbers it is a portrait of attention.</p>
      </div>
      {% renderTemplate "webc" %}
        <editorial-plate class-name="plate--flower float" src="/img/flower.jpeg" alt="A pale flower opening into shadow" folio="Plate 00" caption="A brief clock, opening."></editorial-plate>
      {% endrenderTemplate %}
    </div>
    <blockquote class="overture__thesis split">Every calendar answers two questions: <em>what returns?</em> and <em>what must not be forgotten?</em></blockquote>
  </section>

  <section class="index-banner" aria-label="Essay movements">
